            <ArticleTitle>Pediatric retroperitoneoscopic nephrectomy: An initial experience of 15 cases.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>115-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.4103/0970-9185.150560</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Retroperitoneoscopic approach of nephrectomy in pediatric patients is a debatable issue from surgical point of view. Experience of anesthetic management of 15 such patients from a tertiary care teaching hospital has been described here. We found that capno-retroperitoneum increases end-tidal carbon-di-oxide, but normocapnea was achieved in the most of the patients. No significant hemodynamic changes were noted in any patients. However, subcutaneous emphysema was common, but self-limiting without any serious consequence. Postoperative pain after this procedure is usually manageable by nonopioid analgesics.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
